      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/moenkhausia-copei-4/",
      "html": "<p>Moenkhausia copei reaches a total length of about 6 cm. It is a peaceful and very lively shoaling fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/moenkhausia-copei-4/",
      "html": "<p>Moenkhausia copei is widespread in the Amazon and Orinoco basins. It was first imported in 2011 from Peru, and a colour variation was later received from Venezuela.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/moenkhausia-copei-4/",
      "html": "<p>The Venezuelan fish look a bit like a stretched version of Moenkhausia collettii, where the shoulder spot so typical for M. collettii is missing and where the orange tail fin of M. collettii is red. The Peruvian fish were not quite as strongly coloured.</p><p>In spawning mood, the males develop a strong humeral spot very well. This characteristic can therefore not be used reliably to distinguish between M. collettii and M. copei. The general body form is probably the most reliable distinguishing characteristic.</p>"
    }
  ],
